well if you're feeling a bit narky this  Monday morning this is gonna put a smile  on your face almost 8000 meters below  the surface of the Pacific Ocean  somewhere off the coast of Peru a new  type of smiling fish has been discovered  it's officially called a snail fish and  scientists say that unlike most deep-sea  creatures it hasn't got those gnashing  teeth or those creepy goggly eyes is  actually quite an attractive fish and  science correspondent Thomas More as  we're taking a closer look the Pacific  Ocean off the coast of Peru and this  camera equipment is on a four-hour  freefall to the deep almost 8,000 meters  down it hits the bottom of the Atacama  Trench and reveals life that's never  been seen before a new species of snail  fish they're no more than 30 centimetres  long with barely a bone in their  translucent bodies the immense pressure  of the water holds the gelatinous flesh  together they go against the mold of  what you'd imagine for a deep-sea fish  you know there's no gnashing teeth  there's no creepy bulbous eyes and  they're sort of small pink jelly like  fish they look as if they're smiling  they've got quite a playful wave that  they swim around so I found quite  endearing and I think it's useful to see  you know the world's deepest fish is is  quite an attractive animal it's quite  it's quite a beautiful animal the team  from Newcastle University found three  new species of snail fish a family named  after a shallow-water cousin that bears  a closer resemblance to the mollusk  despite the perpetual darkness and  temperatures just above freezing it's  not a bad place to live  this deep there beyond the reach of  predators and there are plenty of small  creatures living on the seabed for them  to feed on ever more robust equipment is  allowing scientists to take cameras to  parts of the ocean they couldn't reach  before and it's a secret world that's  full of surprises Thomas More Sky News
 

